# Shell style

Source of truth: `../THE-NEXUS/04-STANDARDS/code-style/bash.md`.

## Bash

Header on every script:

```bash
#!/usr/bin/env bash
set -euo pipefail
IFS=$'\n\t'
```

- `shellcheck`-clean.
- Quote every variable: `"$var"`.
- `mktemp` for temp files; `trap` for cleanup.
- Long options when scripting; short only interactive.
- Logs to stderr.
- Errors with meaningful exit codes.

## PowerShell (7+)

Header:

```powershell
#Requires -Version 7.0
[CmdletBinding()]
param(
  [Parameter(Mandatory)][string]$Project,
  [string]$Env = "dev"
)
$ErrorActionPreference = "Stop"
Set-StrictMode -Version Latest
```

- Approved verbs (`Get-Verb`).
- `try { } catch { } finally { }` for cleanup.
- Splat parameters for clarity.
- `Set-Content -Encoding UTF8` for files read by other tools.

## Cross-shell

- If a script must run on both, prefer Python.
- `.gitattributes` enforces LF for `*.sh`/`*.bash`, CRLF for `*.ps1`.

## Forbidden

- Unquoted variables (bash).
- `eval` / `Invoke-Expression` on user input.
- Aliases (`gci`, `%`, `?`) in scripts.
- Parsing `ls` / cmdlet text output instead of using objects.
- Inline secrets.
